WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.020
Two different failures can raise blood glucose.

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:04.628
Start with the pancreatic beta cells.

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:08.230
In type one diabetes, beta cells are destroyed or

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:10.184
lost, and insulin production falls.

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:12.460
Watch the declining insulin supply.

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:17.105
In type two diabetes,
insulin resistance and decreased beta-cell

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:18.846
secretion are the major mechanisms.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:22.920
The liver, muscle, and
adipose tissue respond less effectively

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:23.606
to insulin.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:26.840
Seeing insulin present does not prove that its effect

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:27.480
is adequate.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:30.584
Blood glucose can remain elevated in both comparisons.

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:33.065
The common finding is hyperglycemia.

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:36.400
The question is whether
the problem is secretion, tissue

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:37.485
response, or both.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.238
Keep diabetes mellitus
separate from diabetes insipidus.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:43.669
Mellitus concerns insulin and glucose.

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:48.457
The insipidus lesson concerns
antidiuretic hormone and urine concentration.
